    "help_fix_title" => "When something goes wrong",
    "help_start_linux_q" => "How do I install it on Linux?",
    "help_start_linux_a" => "Take the <b>AppImage</b> if you are not sure: <code>chmod +x dopeIPTV-*.AppImage</code> and run it — nothing to install, and it works on any distribution. There is a <b>.deb</b> for Debian and Ubuntu (<code>sudo apt install ./dopeiptv_*.deb</code>) and a <b>.flatpak</b>. Every build carries Python, Qt, libmpv and ffmpeg inside it, so there are no dependencies to chase.",
    "help_start_macos_q" => "How do I install it on macOS?",
    "help_start_macos_a" => "Open the <code>.dmg</code> and drag dopeIPTV to Applications. It needs macOS 15 or later and runs on Apple Silicon; version 1.2.10 was the last one with an Intel build. The first launch is blocked because the app is not notarised — see the Gatekeeper answer under “When something goes wrong”.",
    "help_start_windows_q" => "How do I install it on Windows?",
    "help_start_windows_a" => "There is no installer: unzip <code>dopeIPTV-*-windows-x64.zip</code> anywhere and run <code>dopeiptv.exe</code>. No admin rights needed. There is a native ARM64 build too, for Snapdragon laptops. A Start-menu shortcut can be made from <b>Settings → Interface → Maintenance → Create shortcut</b>.",
    "help_start_playlist_q" => "How do I add my provider?",
    "help_start_playlist_a" => "On first launch you are asked for the server, username and password of your <b>Xtream Codes</b> account — the server looks like <code>http://example.com:8080</code>. They are saved, and you can add more accounts later under <b>Settings → Playlists</b> and switch between them while the app runs.",
    "help_start_m3u_q" => "Can I use an M3U playlist instead?",
    "help_start_m3u_a" => "Yes. Choose M3U when adding a playlist and give it the URL. Xtream accounts get more out of the app — catch-up, account details, the provider's own guide — but an M3U list plays and can be given its own XMLTV guide URL.",
    "help_start_demo_q" => "Can I try it without an account?",
    "help_start_demo_a" => "Yes. The wizard offers <b>demo channels</b>: a handful of free, public test streams so you can see the app work before entering anything. They are official test assets, not anybody's TV service.",
    "help_watch_play_q" => "How do I play something?",
    "help_watch_play_a" => "Double-click it in the list, or press <b>▶ Play</b> in the panel on the right. Picking another channel switches the stream in place, which is what makes zapping quick.",
    "help_watch_seek_q" => "How do I skip forward and back?",
    "help_watch_seek_a" => "For films, episodes and catch-up: hover the picture and a scrubber slides up, with the timeline and −10s / +30s. Or click the video and use the <b>arrow keys</b> — tap for ten seconds, hold to keep going. Live channels are not seekable and show no scrubber.",
    "help_watch_fullscreen_q" => "Fullscreen?",
    "help_watch_fullscreen_a" => "Double-click the picture, or press <b>F</b>. <b>Esc</b> leaves it. The controls and the pointer hide themselves while you watch, and there is an explicit “Exit fullscreen” button in the floating bar.",
    "help_watch_popout_q" => "Can I move the video to another screen?",
    "help_watch_popout_a" => "Yes — pop it out into its own window. It is frameless by default; drag anywhere on the picture to move it, and right-click for always-on-top or a title bar. <b>Esc</b>, or right-click → Exit, brings it home.",
    "help_watch_tracks_q" => "Audio language and subtitles?",
    "help_watch_tracks_a" => "The <b>⚙</b> button in the player bar picks the audio track and the subtitle track, and also holds audio delay, aspect ratio, the network buffer and “Stats for nerds”. A choice you make is remembered, and for a series it carries over to the next episode.",
    "help_watch_keys_q" => "What are the keyboard shortcuts?",
    "help_watch_keys_a" => "<b>Space</b> pauses, <b>F</b> is fullscreen, <b>Esc</b> leaves it, <b>←/→</b> seek and <b>M</b> mutes. They work in fullscreen and in the popped-out window too.",
    "help_watch_resume_q" => "Does it remember where I stopped?",
    "help_watch_resume_a" => "Films, episodes, recordings and your own files do. Starting one again asks whether to resume or start over. Positions very near the start or the end are ignored, so it does not offer to resume something you had just begun or just finished.",
    "help_epg_what_q" => "Where does the guide come from?",
    "help_epg_what_a" => "From your provider. Live channels show a now-playing line with a progress bar, and the guide is cached on disk per playlist so it appears instantly the next time you start — even before the fresh copy has downloaded.",
    "help_epg_guide_q" => "Is there a full guide?",
    "help_epg_guide_a" => "Yes: the <b>Guide</b> button opens a searchable programme grid for every channel, with a timeline you can scroll. Double-clicking a past programme on a catch-up channel plays it.",
    "help_epg_xmltv_q" => "My provider's guide is wrong or missing.",
    "help_epg_xmltv_a" => "Give the playlist its own guide URL: <b>Settings → Playlists</b>, edit the playlist, and set a custom <b>XMLTV</b> address. It overrides whatever the provider sends. The app also falls back through several sources by itself and matches channels by id or by name.",
    "help_epg_refresh_q" => "How do I force a refresh?",
    "help_epg_refresh_a" => "<b>Settings → EPG cache → Refresh EPG</b> re-downloads the guide with a progress bar. It also refreshes on its own when the current programme ends, and each playlist can be set to refresh at startup or every few hours.",
    "help_epg_times_q" => "The listings are shifted by an hour or two.",
    "help_epg_times_a" => "Some providers publish times that do not match what they actually broadcast. <b>Settings → Playback</b> has an <b>EPG delay</b> that shifts every displayed time, and a separate <b>Replay delay</b> for where catch-up starts playing.",
    "help_ts_what_q" => "What is timeshift?",
    "help_ts_what_a" => "Some providers keep a recording of each channel for a few days. Where they do, the channel gets a <b>⏪</b> marker in the list and the detail panel says how many days deep the archive goes.",
    "help_ts_start_q" => "How do I watch a programme that has already started?",
    "help_ts_start_a" => "Press the <b>⏪</b> button in the player, or right-click the channel → Timeshift, and choose “from the start”. The archive plays like a film — you can seek inside it.",
    "help_ts_browse_q" => "Can I go back to yesterday?",
    "help_ts_browse_a" => "Yes. The timeshift menu lists past programmes grouped by day; double-click one to watch it. You can also jump back in steps, which scale to how deep your provider's archive actually is — from thirty minutes up to a week.",
    "help_ts_depth_q" => "Timeshift says it is not available.",
    "help_ts_depth_a" => "Then the provider does not flag an archive for that channel. It is their setting, not the app's. Some providers accept an archive request and quietly serve the live feed instead; the app notices that and tells you rather than pretending.",
    "help_rec_start_q" => "How do I record?",
    "help_rec_start_a" => "Press the <b>REC</b> button in the player, or right-click a channel → Record. It records until you stop it unless you choose otherwise.",
    "help_rec_timed_q" => "Can I record for a set length?",
    "help_rec_timed_a" => "Yes — 30 minutes, 1, 2 or 4 hours from the REC menu.",
    "help_rec_scheduled_q" => "Can I schedule a recording?",
    "help_rec_scheduled_a" => "Yes, with a start and a stop time. Scheduled jobs survive a restart of the app, but the app has to be running when they fire — it is not a service.",
    "help_rec_oneconn_q" => "Will recording use a second connection?",
    "help_rec_oneconn_a" => "Not if you record the channel you are watching: it taps the player's own stream, so it works on the usual one-stream-at-a-time accounts. Recording a <em>different</em> channel, or a scheduled job firing later, does open its own connection.",
    "help_rec_cap_q" => "Can I stop a recording running away with my disk?",
    "help_rec_cap_a" => "Yes. <b>Settings → Recording</b> takes a size limit for every recording, and the REC menu has a quick per-session cap from 250 MB to 10 GB.",
    "help_rec_where_q" => "Where do the files go?",
    "help_rec_where_a" => "Wherever you choose in <b>Settings → Recording</b>. The <b>Recordings</b> section in the sidebar browses them, with folders as categories, renaming, moving and multi-select delete.",
    "help_rec_while_q" => "Can I watch a recording while it is still recording?",
    "help_rec_while_a" => "Yes. Note that an in-player recording is finalised when the recording stops — stopping playback, zapping away or a stream error ends it.",
    "help_mv_open_q" => "What is multiview?",
    "help_mv_open_a" => "A separate grid window showing 2, 4, 6 or 9 live channels at once, each an independent stream. They can come from different playlists and accounts.",
    "help_mv_audio_q" => "Which one do I hear?",
    "help_mv_audio_a" => "Click a window to give it the audio; a red border marks the one you are hearing. Double-click maximises a window.",
    "help_mv_options_q" => "Can I change a single window?",
    "help_mv_options_a" => "Right-click it: mute, audio and subtitle track, move or swap it with another, or remove it. A catch-up channel gets its own archive timeline inside its window.",
    "help_mv_cost_q" => "Does multiview use several connections?",
    "help_mv_cost_a" => "Yes — one per window. Most IPTV accounts allow only one or two at a time, so nine windows will be refused by most providers. The app says so once, before you find out the hard way.",
    "help_local_add_q" => "Can I watch my own files?",
    "help_local_add_a" => "Yes. <b>Local files</b> in the sidebar browses folders you add under <b>Settings → Local files</b>. A network share your system has already mounted appears by itself — these are ordinary paths, so there is no SMB client in the app to go wrong.",
    "help_local_views_q" => "Folders or a library?",
    "help_local_views_a" => "Both, toggled in the middle column. <b>Folders</b> shows the tree as it is on disk. <b>Library</b> reads the file names and builds series with seasons and episodes, with films under Movies.",
    "help_local_art_q" => "Where do the posters come from?",
    "help_local_art_a" => "TMDB, for anything that can be matched by name and year. A file with no year and no release tag is never guessed at — it gets a frame from the video instead of some unrelated film's poster.",
    "help_local_music_q" => "Does it play music?",
    "help_local_music_a" => "Yes. Albums are shelved together, artist and title come from the tags, and there is a play queue — add a track, queue a whole folder, or play something next. It moves on by itself when a track ends.",
    "help_local_missing_q" => "A file in History will not play.",
    "help_local_missing_a" => "It has moved, been renamed, or its share is not mounted. History says so plainly and offers to drop the entry rather than failing silently.",
    "help_cast_start_q" => "How do I cast to a Chromecast?",
    "help_cast_start_a" => "Right-click a channel, film or episode → <b>Cast to Chromecast…</b>, and pick the device. Live channels are sent as HLS. Casting stops local playback, and a strip above the player shows what is being cast and lets you stop it.",
    "help_cast_convert_q" => "The TV refuses a channel.",
    "help_cast_convert_a" => "Some channels are ordinary video with Dolby Digital Plus audio, which a Chromecast that is not an Ultra or a Google TV cannot decode. The app converts those on this machine — the video is copied through untouched, only the audio is re-encoded — and serves the result on your own network. That only happens after the device has actually refused the stream.",
    "help_cast_stop_q" => "How do I stop casting?",
    "help_cast_stop_a" => "The stop button on the cast strip, or simply play something in the app — that hands playback back and releases the second connection.",
    "help_trakt_connect_q" => "How do I connect Trakt?",
    "help_trakt_connect_a" => "<b>Settings → Trakt</b>. You create an application on trakt.tv, paste the two keys, and authorise in the browser.",
    "help_trakt_scrobble_q" => "What does it sync?",
    "help_trakt_scrobble_a" => "What you watch is scrobbled, and your watchlist and history can be browsed in the app. Your own local files are scrobbled too, when TMDB could name the file.",
    "help_set_language_q" => "Can I change the language?",
    "help_set_language_a" => "<b>Settings → Interface</b>. The interface is translated into 27 languages and switches immediately, with no restart. Arabic, Persian, Hebrew and Urdu mirror the whole layout.",
    "help_set_theme_q" => "Themes?",
    "help_set_theme_a" => "Five colour themes and seven accent colours, also under Interface, applied live.",
    "help_set_playback_q" => "Can I set defaults for every stream?",
    "help_set_playback_a" => "<b>Settings → Playback</b>: preferred audio language, subtitles on or off with a preferred language, default aspect ratio and network buffer. The ⚙ menu still overrides them for the current stream.",
    "help_set_subs_q" => "Subtitles are off by default and I want them on.",
    "help_set_subs_a" => "Set that under <b>Settings → Playback</b> — subtitles on, plus a first and second preferred language.",
    "help_set_parental_q" => "Can I lock categories?",
    "help_set_parental_a" => "Yes. A PIN protects locked categories and locked favourite groups; a locked group does not reveal what is in it until the PIN is entered.",
    "help_set_hide_q" => "Can I hide channels I never watch?",
    "help_set_hide_a" => "Right-click any channel, film or series to rename or hide it — effectively your own edited playlist, saved per playlist. “Restore default channels” puts everything back exactly as the provider sends it. Whole categories can be hidden, renamed or locked from the category right-click menu.",
    "help_set_playlists_q" => "Several providers at once?",
    "help_set_playlists_a" => "Yes. Add as many playlists as you like and switch at runtime; favourites and history are kept separately per playlist, and each can have its own guide URL and auto-refresh schedule.",
    "help_fix_start_q" => "The app will not start.",
    "help_fix_start_a" => "Run it from a terminal and read what it prints — that names the cause far more often than not. On Linux the AppImage needs nothing installed; if you are running from source you need <code>mpv</code> and <code>ffmpeg</code> from your distribution.",
    "help_fix_picture_q" => "Sound but no picture.",
    "help_fix_picture_a" => "That is the embedded player failing to get a video surface. <b>Settings → Playback</b> has an X11 option on Wayland that is worth trying, and <code>⚙ → Stats for nerds</code> shows whether frames are arriving at all.",
    "help_fix_gatekeeper_q" => "macOS says the app cannot be opened.",
    "help_fix_gatekeeper_a" => "It is not notarised by Apple yet. <b>Right-click the app → Open</b>, then Open in the dialog — or allow it under <b>System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Open Anyway</b>. If macOS says it is “damaged”, clear the download flag: <code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/dopeIPTV.app</code>. That warning only means the build is not code-signed.",
    "help_fix_smartscreen_q" => "Windows says “Windows protected your PC”.",
    "help_fix_smartscreen_a" => "SmartScreen does not recognise the publisher, because the build is not code-signed — that needs a paid certificate. Click <b>More info → Run anyway</b>.",
    "help_fix_defender_q" => "Defender deleted dopeiptv.exe.",
    "help_fix_defender_a" => "A false positive, and largely fixed in 1.2.10: the launcher is compiled from source rather than shipped prebuilt, and the exe now carries a proper version resource. If your engine still flags it, allow the folder and report the false positive to Microsoft — that is what clears it for everyone.",
    "help_fix_stream_q" => "A channel will not play.",
    "help_fix_stream_a" => "The app probes the provider and tells you why in plain language — account expired, connection limit reached, stream missing, server unreachable — instead of a bare failure. If it says the connection limit is reached, something else is using your account: another device, or multiview.",
    "help_fix_limit_q" => "What does “connection limit reached” mean?",
    "help_fix_limit_a" => "Your account allows a certain number of streams at once and they are all in use. Close multiview, stop a recording of a different channel, or check another device. Recording the channel you are watching does not count as a second connection.",
    "help_fix_icon_q" => "On Linux the taskbar shows a blank icon.",
    "help_fix_icon_a" => "Wayland takes a window's icon from a desktop entry, and an AppImage has none until something installs one. The app offers to add one the first time it starts; say yes, then restart it. The switch also lives under <b>Settings → Playback</b>. If the icon still does not appear, delete <code>~/.local/share/icons/hicolor/icon-theme.cache</code> — a stale one hides icons added since it was built.",
    "help_fix_external_q" => "“Open externally” does nothing.",
    "help_fix_external_a" => "That opens a standalone <code>mpv</code> or <code>vlc</code> from your system, so one of them has to be installed. The built-in player needs nothing extra.",
    "help_fix_logs_q" => "Where is the log?",
    "help_fix_logs_a" => "Start with <code>DOPEIPTV_LOG_FILE=/tmp/dopeiptv.log</code> set and it writes one there; <code>DOPEIPTV_LOG=debug</code> adds the verbose traces. Attach it to a GitHub issue — credentials are masked out of it automatically.",
];
